    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d149">

                <pb facs="0405" n="365" sameAs="frus:frus1951v05/pg365"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">774.00/5–251: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Egypt</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Department of State
                    </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d149-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1951-05-02T13:00:00+02:00">May 2, 1951—1 p.
                            m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1123. Egyptian Chamber Deputies “got even” last night with Brit Parl for
                    anti-Egyptian statements. In strongest language yet used during Anglo-Egyptian
                    negots Wafd as well as opposition speakers attacked Churchill and Conservative in particular Brit in gen, demanded
                    abrogation 1936 Treaty, boycott to Brit, and treatment to “Brit dogs” in canal
                    zone as “enemies”. One speaker went so far as to call for non-aggression treaty
                    with Russia, another melodramatically tore up copy 1936 Treaty to tumultuous
                    applause from house. US attacked as ally Brit ME
                    policy.</p>
                <p>In reply FonMin
                    Salaheddin demonstrated maximum
                    self-restraint possible under circumstances, promised any new treaty wld be
                    submitted Parl before signature and that he wld never sign an agreement that did
                    not realize Egypt’s national demands to the full. “Either Brit concedes our
                    demands or the result will be what you have suggested”.</p>
                <p>
                    FonMin made three points in particular: (1)
                    Defending restrictions Suez Canal tankers;<anchor n="2" xml:id="d149-f2"/> (2)
                    Condemning Brit failure permit supply arms including threat to “buy them from
                    the devil if necessary”; and (3) Reiterating usual Sudan stand. Overall effect
                    his speech calming in that no new definite Egyptian action any kind
                    announced.</p>
                <p>Altho mtg probably inevitable “escape valve” for pent-up Egyptian feelings, any
                    definite dragging our negots certain become progressively more difficult.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d149-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d149-f1-content">
                <p>Repeated to London, Beirut for Blandford,
                    and all other Arab capitals.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d149-f2" n="2" xml:id="d149-f2-content">
                <p>Reference is to Egyptian restrictions against tankers using the Suez Canal as a
                    passageway to Israel ports. For documentation on this subject, see <ref
                        target="#pg559">pp. 559</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
